### TITLE ###
RegexQuantifierSilentlyInterpolates
### DESCRIPTION ###
The case RAD40017 exists for: `\d{4}` reads as a regex quantifier but
parses as "a digit, then the number 4", producing `\d4` with no error
anywhere. Multi-part quantifiers (`{2,3}`) are already a syntax error,
so this single-number form is the only silent one.
### INPUT ###
pattern = "\d{4}"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  pattern @ 1:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  pattern (local): str

# Diagnostics
  [warn] RAD40017 @ 1:14 - Interpolating the literal 4 has no effect
    help: Write the value directly. For a literal brace, escape it as '\{' or use a raw string: r"..."

### TITLE ###
EveryConstantLiteralKind
### DESCRIPTION ###
int, float, bool and simple-string literals all interpolate to exactly
what the author typed, so all four warn.
### INPUT ###
a = "{0}"
b = "{3.14}"
c = "{true}"
d = "{'s'}"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  a @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  b @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  c @ 3:1 -> <no-type>
  d @ 4:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  a (local): str
  b (local): str
  c (local): str
  d (local): str

# Diagnostics
  [warn] RAD40017 @ 1:6 - Interpolating the literal 0 has no effect
    help: Write the value directly. For a literal brace, escape it as '\{' or use a raw string: r"..."
  [warn] RAD40017 @ 2:6 - Interpolating the literal 3.14 has no effect
    help: Write the value directly. For a literal brace, escape it as '\{' or use a raw string: r"..."
  [warn] RAD40017 @ 3:6 - Interpolating the literal true has no effect
    help: Write the value directly. For a literal brace, escape it as '\{' or use a raw string: r"..."
  [warn] RAD40017 @ 4:6 - Interpolating the literal 's' has no effect
    help: Write the value directly. For a literal brace, escape it as '\{' or use a raw string: r"..."

### TITLE ###
NonLiteralExpressionsStayQuiet
### DESCRIPTION ###
The rule is syntactic, not const-folding: a variable holding a constant,
an arithmetic expression over two literals, and a call all stay quiet.
Widening past this buys false positives, which is the failure mode the
checker is already being cleaned up for.
### INPUT ###
digits = 4
fn four():
    return 4
x = "{digits}"
y = "{2 + 2}"
z = "{four()}"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  digits @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  x @ 4:1 -> <no-type>
  digits @ 4:7 -> <no-type>
  y @ 5:1 -> <no-type>
  z @ 6:1 -> <no-type>
  four @ 6:7 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  digits (local): int
  four (fn): fn() -> int
  x (local): str
  y (local): str
  z (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
FormatSpecWidthsAreNotValues
### DESCRIPTION ###
Regression guard. Format padding and precision are int literals hanging
off the same interpolation node, and LitString.Children() yields them
alongside the value expression - so a check written over the generic
child list fires on every padded format spec instead of on nothing.
### INPUT ###
name = "ana"
pi = 3.14
a = "{name:<12}|"
b = "{pi:.2}"
c = "{name:*>8}"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  name @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  pi @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  a @ 3:1 -> <no-type>
  name @ 3:7 -> <no-type>
  b @ 4:1 -> <no-type>
  pi @ 4:7 -> <no-type>
  c @ 5:1 -> <no-type>
  name @ 5:7 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  a (local): str
  b (local): str
  c (local): str
  name (local): str
  pi (local): float

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
BothEscapeHatchesStayQuiet
### DESCRIPTION ###
The two exits the diagnostic points at must not themselves warn: `\{`
escapes the brace, and a raw string suppresses interpolation entirely.
### INPUT ###
escaped = "\d\{4}"
raw = r"\d{4}"
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  escaped @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  raw @ 2:1 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  escaped (local): str
  raw (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)
